sson that  I've learned that I think we haven't  done a very good job in terms of the  rhetoric that's been political rhetoric  that's been used and some condescending  comments made by the administration in  general about the border and more  specifically about the Mexican people  those things can really ruin  relationships that took years to  maintain and build so with the El Chapo  case the trafficking of illicit drugs  was a big factor in terms of how El  Chapo was funded the networks that he  had in many many different countries can  you talk a little bit about I mean you  were front and center in this  investigation the drugs that were being  trafficked what percentage can you even  estimate we're coming over through the  border but not through ports of entry  rather where there might be a wall if we  were to build one I would say maybe 20%  and here's how I know that through many  many investigations through the briefing  of cooperating that defendants through  informant information through working  with the Mexicans through legal  electronic surveillance where we're  actually hearing the bad guys talk to  the bad guys on both sides of the border  there's very little evidence that the  large cartels are willing to move  high-volume  amounts of drugs in desolate on wild  areas when they can it makes better  business sense for them to attack the  ports of entry where literally thousands  of cars trucks and buses and people  cross every single day was there a  particular policy and of course you were  working on this case for some long  period of time so policy shifted and  changed during that time but was there  any particular policy enacted by the  federal government that helped you in  the work you were doing on this case and  was there any federal government policy  that may be hindered or slowed down your  progress on this case I think quite  frankly the State Department was really  instrumental in what  talk about bridge building and also  establishing institutions within some of  these countries and really trying to  leverage the rule of law in favor for  our investigations and the State  Department spent I don't know what their  anti-drug budget was I know ambassador  bill brownfield who is a dear friend of  mine used to head that up and I know it  was over a billion dollars so our  ability to help them train to get them  equipment and to make them interface  with us in other countries so that we  can share intelligence information and  make kids both domestically and in  Mexico and coordinate that information I  think has been one of the real  highlights over the last 20 years so  before we get into some of the  nitty-gritty of the case because it's  it's super interesting the last sort of  pseudo political thing I wanted to ask  about was when you hear about the idea  of taking money that DoD would normally  have for use on the drug issue that you  worked on taking it and instead using  some portion of that to build a wall  does that seem like a good idea to you  not a good idea or you're unsure it  scares me quite quite frankly if you  looked at the proposal where they're  getting the money obviously it's thick  and I think a billion dollars from the  State Department's anti-narcotic efforts  and it's also taking 600 million dollars  from the drug asset forfeiture fund and  that's what really scares me because  much of what we do here domestically is  done through our task force's and that's  where we go out and this is not just DEA  it's all federal agencies we go out and  we deputize state and local policemen to  sit and work shoulder their shoulder  with us on task forces and their  overtime is paid from that fund their  training is paid from from that fund so  I haven't seen the specific chip but  that was the one thing that really  alarmed me because it's almost like  robbing Peter to pay Paul if our task  forces shrink our intelligence based  shrinks our resources to work cases  shrink and we begin to lose touch with  our state and local agencies who are  vital to everything  do I think right now the way we are yeah  I think we almost have an evil even  number of DEA special agents federal  agents and deputized task forces so you  can imagine if that I wrote it at all  it's gonna really hurt our ability to do  the job point point point taken  okay so let's talk a little bit about  your work on the case there are lots of  people who work on cases like this who  remain relatively anonymous particularly  to the people that are on the other side  of their sort of interest because you  were known as the El Paso special agent  in charge for a period of time you  weren't able to do that in other words  the adversary knew exactly who you were  was that a problem and did it become a  problem in your personal life at any  point in time well I'll tell you there  was a couple of incidents that we had in  El Paso and then when I moved on to  become the chief operations and then the  number two at the EA they taught me a  very valuable lesson the safety issue of  our agents along the border and really  all over the world it was the real thing  that kept me up at night especially as  the deputy because when you've got phone  calls in the middle of the night and  you're talking about globally it's never  good news so with the heightened  violence at the time I was in El Paso  and wire Juarez made people at that time  we're seeing it was more dangerous than  Kabul and and Baghdad it really was a  concern and we had to really take a  special special care of our civilian  employees many of which who lived right  on the board and we had several they  actually lived in water so my concern  was for their safety and certainly the  kidnapping that was going on along the  border and still in some parts of the  border today was it was a real concern  were you specifically targeted to your  knowledge at any point for your  participation in that investigation yeah  I think in 2007 when I first 