g  an issue and obviously leading the  opioid dependence we know that chap was  slowed down his importation of cocaine  and sped up and spent enormous resources  to produce more poppies in Mexico so he  saw the market turning and what he did  was I think unfortunately he's a mass  murderer no question about it he's one  hell of a corporate CEO what he did was  he saw the market and he changed his  business model to meet the market so he  began flooding the US  with cheap high potency heroin and this  was different heroin because it was  being smoked and snorted there wasn't a  needle there wasn't the fear of AIDS or  hepatitis so it attracted a completely  different user base you know on down to  our high schools and professional adults  almost glamourizing it as a weekend drug  but what was really important was at  that point the opioid crisis took over  and eventually your doctor stops writing  prescription the pharmacist won't give  it to you difficult difficult to get on  the street you can't get it out of your  grandmother's medicine cabinet anymore  because it's gone so what do you do well  you gotta walk that dark road to buying  heroin in Chicago choppa was brilliant  because he used nearly a hundred  thousand street gang members really is  his co-conspirators his unwitting  salesmen if you will who put the dope on  the street and collected money and he  could care less who they kill as they  did their business so when I look at  chapo and Sinaloa I I've never seen  anything as corporately organized now  chapo also and it's part of his downfall  he was a master manipulator  he was extremely paranoid very paranoid  about telecommunications and he was just  a taskmaster he was way too micromanager  he was involved in things he should have  never been involved in in terms of  negotiating things and putting him and  his organization in harm's way and that  was one of the reasons why we were able  to really indict him in eight or nine  jurisdiction here in the United States  and then bring it all together for the  trial here in New York at the level of  the DEA at which you operated  particularly towards the end of your  career were their candid discussions  between you and colleagues where you  would say you know we we might solve a  lot of problems if we legalized cannabis  I know I mean that's not to say that  people weren't thinking that especially  under the Obama administration where you  began seeing the state's you know  exploring and taking responsibility for  but I was a cop I wasn't a politician in  my last  two years there I spent a tremendous  amount of time testifying in front of  Congress and working on the bill with  senators and staffers and congressmen  and it was extremely frustrating because  I heard from them why don't you just  forget marijuana just abandon it you  know and go after the hard drugs well  the problem was and I used to get  people's upset so there's a cop you make  the laws if you tell us today DEA you're  responsible for arresting people that  sell widgets well that's what I'm gonna  do but if you're asking me as if there's  a cop to make these decisions not only  in that qualified DEA is not qualified  to do it it's based off the science so  everyone blames the EEA saying well they  won't you know marijuana still scheduled  water it's not us it's the FDA it's the  scientists that say there's no human use  for medical use for so I think there's  so much misinformation and there's a the  lobbying groups for legalization and I  think very effective and certainly the  men bankrolled by some billionaire so  this is a tough problem we're facing now  and one of the reasons I didn't want to  write the book to be honest with you but  one of the reasons I wanted to write it  was to try to kind of explain how we got  here yeah and the and the people in  which we have to deal with that are  terrible and if we don't deal with them  if we don't maintain a strong robust  international law enforcement 